        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "ebe599e2-7ad2-5894-bae5-28490438e489",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: Nol Card, Octopus card, FeliCa\n- Places: Dubai, Hong Kong, Singapore, Japan, United States, London\n- Concepts: electronic ticketing, contactless stored value smart cards, public transport payment systems\n\nFacts:\n- The Nol Card is an electronic ticketing card for all modes of public transport in Dubai, and it was released in August 2009.\n- The Octopus card is a reusable contactless stored value smart card for electronic payments in online or offline systems in Hong Kong.\n- The Octopus card system was launched in Hong Kong in September 1997 to collect fares for the territory's mass transit system.\n- The Octopus card system was the world's second contactless smart card system after the Korean Upass.\n- The Octopus card later became a widely used payment system for all public transport in Hong Kong and influenced the development of London's Oyster Card.\n- FeliCa technology was first utilized in the Octopus card system in Hong Kong.\n- FeliCa technology is also used in a variety of cards in Singapore, Japan, and the United States.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "41f02553-eb2d-5c8e-b020-1b2418b70e4a",
        "text": "Page: Nol Card. Sentence: The Nol Card is an electronic ticketing card that was released in for all mode of public transport in Dubai services, on August 2009.\nPage: Octopus card. Sentence: The Octopus card () is a reusable contactless stored value smart card for making electronic payments in online or offline systems in Hong Kong.\nPage: Octopus card. Sentence: Launched in September 1997 to collect fares for the territory's mass transit system, the Octopus card system is the second contactless smart card system in the world, after the Korean Upass, and has since grown into a widely used payment system for all public transport in Hong Kong, leading to the development of Oyster Card in London.\nPage: FeliCa. Sentence: First utilized in the Octopus card system in Hong Kong, the technology is used in a variety of cards also in countries such as Singapore, Japan and the United States.",
